{"id": "live-340", "title": "In re Jon Venetos: Lenders File Involuntary Chapter 7 Petition Against Lurin Capital Founder", "category": "lending-foreclosure", "status": "filed", "date": "2026-09-17", "jurisdiction": "U.S. Bankruptcy Court for the Southern District of Texas", "state": "TX", "amount": "$33.7M in petitioning-creditor judgments behind the involuntary petition; separate $19M guaranty judgment to Acore Capital Mortgage", "source": "live", "source_url": "https://therealdeal.com/texas/2026/09/30/jon-venetos-creditors-file-involuntary-chapter-7-bankruptcy/", "summary": "Silver Point Finance, LLC, Vista Bank, and NexPoint Real Estate Finance, Inc. filed an involuntary Chapter 7 bankruptcy petition against Jon Venetos, founder of Dallas-based multifamily syndicator Lurin Capital, on September 17, 2026 in the U.S. Bankruptcy Court for the Southern District of Texas. The petitioning creditors cite a combined roughly $33.7 million in judgments already entered against Venetos personally on guaranties tied to Lurin's defaulted apartment portfolio, a step observers have called one of the rarest moves in bankruptcy.", "significance": "The filing shows how quickly a sponsor's personal guaranty exposure can outrun a corporate Chapter 11 once multiple lenders reduce separate guaranties to judgment, and illustrates that creditors facing a shrinking, overlapping pool of a guarantor's personal assets may find a coordinated involuntary petition more effective than racing each other to collect individually.", "judge": null, "tags": ["involuntary bankruptcy", "personal guaranty", "multifamily", "chapter 7", "syndicator collapse"]}
